\begin{note}
\emph{Quick sort} picks a \emph{pivot} element from the input,
partitions the other elements into those smaller than, equal to,
and greater than the pivot, and then recursively sorts the
``smaller'' and ``larger'' partitions. Unlike merge sort, quick
sort does the partition work \emph{before} recursing, so it is an
example of ``come from'' recursion rather than ``go to''
recursion.

In the average case the pivot splits the input evenly, so quick
sort satisfies the same recurrence as merge sort,
$T(n) = 2 T(n/2) + O(n)$, giving $O(n \log n)$ time. In the worst
case the pivot is always the minimum or maximum element, and quick
sort degrades to $O(n^2)$.

One common bug is including the pivot itself in the ``smaller''
partition; when the pivot appears in the input only once this
makes the recursion fail to shrink, producing a stack overflow.
\end{note}
